{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 28, :comments ["<b>And they said, ra'oh ra'inu, etc.</b> One needs to know why he doubled the expression ״ra'oh״ etc., as well as the rest of the fine points?" "<b>Indeed,</b> they intended to respond to everything that Isaac's words might bear when he said ״why״ etc., and they said: since your intent was already sworn to us by your father — ״ra'oh ra'inu״ etc. — two observations: the first, that the oath of Abraham cannot be relied upon for the reasons we have written above; we have further seen that through your person the oath is fulfilled like cast metal." "We have further seen that your father's oath — we did not scrutinize the legal rulings that void the covenant, as we have written above. Also, if your intent is to say that we did not go to Ishmael to forge a covenant with us, since he is among the heirs of your father and he is the firstborn — we have seen that HaShem is with you, and this is your sign that Abraham's promises belong to you and you are the principal in them. Also, if your intent is to accept [our apology] for having shown you hatred by sending you away — we have seen that HaShem is with you, and in the case of a man such as this, wherever he goes there he is guarded and successful, and his standing does not change from place to place — so there is no hatred here." "<b>Also,</b> regarding what might be the vexation over what was revealed from our hearts — that [we acted] not uprightly with you — and you would say, how can you request good from me? For this too an answer came in their saying <b>ra'oh ra'inu</b> — an intellectual seeing, without which the oath between us would not be established with full legal force, as they were precise in their words, as I shall explain below:" "<b>And we said, let there be now, etc.</b> One needs to know [why] he said ״and we said״ — in the past tense. They began to give a reason for why they sent him away: for previously, when he was with them under [their] authority, the oath could not be valid, on the basis of what the Sages, whose memory is for blessing, said in Nedarim (folio 27), and this is their language: ״One may vow to murderers and excommunicators, and Beit Hillel say: even under oath.״ And the Maharik wrote in section 282, and this is his language: ״One who vows or swears under compulsion — it is not a vow and not an oath, etc., and he thinks in his heart, etc.״ According to this, any oath that would be under the hand of a coercer — the one swearing may nullify it in his heart; and Joseph to Pharaoh and Zedekiah to Nebuchadnezzar did not nullify [it] in their hearts. And this law applies even if he received benefit from him, and with all the force of oath-reinforcements — whenever a strong hand is in the middle, he nullifies in his heart and there is no oath there. Therefore Avimelech etc. was concerned to forge a covenant with Isaac under his authority, since Isaac could nullify it in his heart by the legal principle of ״swearing under compulsion.״ Therefore they contrived to forge a covenant in the place where they were now, where Avimelech had no dominion over him — and this is also implied by the words of the Ramban. And that is [the meaning of] his saying <b>let there be now</b> — meaning: now the imprecation shall be, and not in the prior circumstance, and with this there is no concern for nullification of the oath in the heart. And the reason for their saying ״for you have become too mighty for us״ etc. is a borrowed reason, and he did not wish to reveal the true reason to him, lest he press them hard on account of the reason and suffice with a covenant there and the like; also they had other reasons, as I shall explain in their words — therefore they stated to him a definitive reason." "<b>Between us, between us and you, etc.</b> The reason he lengthened to say 'between us, etc.' is that he intended to strengthen the covenant of Abraham and to add a newly formed covenant with Yitzḥak. The intention in this is to ascertain whether the first covenant of Abraham still stands as a banner, and there is in this a reinforcement of the oath of Yitzḥak, since Abraham has already passed away and there is no validity to its nullification for the sons, and to uphold everything Yitzḥak swears under whatever conditions he stipulates. Or perhaps, because Avimelekh also feared that HaShem might give a gift anew to Yitzḥak, and he bequeaths it to Esav — and Esav is not the seed of Abraham, as it is written (Genesis 21:12): ״כִּי בְיִצְחָק יִקָּרֵא לְךָ זָרַע״ — meaning 'in Yitzḥak' but not all of Yitzḥak; and if Yitzḥak gives the land of the Philistines to Esav, Avimelekh will not win his claim against Esav on the side of the covenant of Abraham, for he will say: this is a new gift that HaShem gave to Yitzḥak after the death of Abraham, and Yitzḥak gave it to Esav, and no one can rescue it from his hand. Therefore he was clever to make Yitzḥak swear, and the oath of Yitzḥak will apply also to Esav, for he is called 'his seed' even though he is not called the seed of Abraham. And the reason he did not mention the generations — they already alluded to them in their saying 'between us, etc.,' and our Sages of blessed memory (Rashi) explained that 'between us, etc.' is there, and there it says (Genesis 21:23): ״לִי וּלְנִינִי וּלְנֶכְדִּי״." "<b>Further,</b> he alludes in saying <b>between us,</b> etc. — meaning that the covenant should be in the presence of the public, not that he make a covenant with Avimelekh between himself and him alone, but between them all of them together, and the intention in this is so that it be in the presence of the public. And they said 'between us' further to allude that the intention should be with their consent, so that it be with the consent of the public."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 26, :notes {4 "The Maharik's section number is given as רל״ב (282) in the text; the standard reference for this Maharik ruling (in his Einot Mi-yeshenot) is conventionally cited as shoresh 282.", 5 "The phrase לָדַעַת בְּרִית הָרִאשׁוֹנָה שֶׁל אַבְרָהָם הַאִם עוֹמֶדֶת לְנֵס is translated hyper-literally; לְנֵס ('as a banner/standard') may carry a more specific idiom here."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:44:02.578938654Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}